AB  - INFORMATION PORTRAYAL is the purpose of radioisotope scanning. The area of interest is displayed on the photoscan either as a "positive" density, due to its accumulation of gamma radioactivity, or as a "negative" density outlined by radioactivity concentrated in surrounding tissues. This information is often of clinical value, but may be incomplete or inconclusive. The yield of useful information is usually increased by correlation of the photoscan with a radiogram of the same area. The validity of such a correlation hinges on avoidance of a number of technical pitfalls associated with both methods. These pitfalls include factors which may magnify, minify, distort, or obscure the recorded outline of the area examined. When the photoscan and radiogram are compared by superimposition, new problems may arise. For example, superimposition of a spleen scan may demonstrate an apparently ectopic or accessory spleen if the radiogram was exposed during maximal expiratory elevation of the. © 1965, American Medical Association. All rights reserved.
